hey everyone it's sarah register nurse  rn.com and in this video i'm going to  give you some quick  tips on how to assess for veins before  you start a peripheral ivy  before performing any nursing skills you  want to make sure that you're following  your hospital's protocols  to ensure you're following the latest  guidelines  now i'm going to assess the patient's  arm in order to find a vein that is good  enough for an iv that i want to insert  in them  so before i do that i want to perform  hand hygiene again before i even touch  the patient and then i want to  grab the tourniquet that i had in my iv  start kit  now applying the tourniquet what it's  going to do is it's going to  put pressure on these veins and cause  them to pop out more  you don't always have to use a  tourniquet if your patient has  just really bulging veins you may not  want to use one because too much  pressure could blow them  this patient has great veins so i'm  going to use a tourniquet very  lightly just to give us a little bit of  increased pressure  so i'm going to place the tourniquet on  the arm  and what you do is you put it around and  it's sort of like tying a shoe so you're  going to take it out  and then you're going to cross it and  then you're going to make this bunny ear  so we're going to go here and just make  a bunny ear  and that is just going to apply pressure  on our veins and the reason we tie it  like this is because we want to be able  to  quickly release it so just do this  and it quickly releases it now whenever  you're looking for veins you want to go  in an  area where you know you're going to find  some veins and in most patients they're  usually going to be in the same spot  so whenever you're first starting out  you need to know what a vein feels like  veins are very springy feeling and they  do not pulsate if it pulsates it's an  artery  and um the best way is you know to feel  on yourself  feel those veins and get an idea of what  it feels like and look for them and just  know where they normally hang out in the  body  so the antecubital area is a great place  to look  there's usually large veins there they  tend to be a little bit deeper  in this area of the area than compared  to let's say the hand  but he has one right here and right here  those are usually  great places especially for drawing  blood and then we can also  look at the forearm and some patients  depending on their disease process  and what they have going on you may find  some  in the forearm if they have a lot of  adipose tissue you may not see them as  well but this patient you can see them  very nicely and then whenever you turn  over the arm you can see them  there as well and then there is the hand  and the hand usually has great veins in  it that are  superficial easy to get to but one thing  you want to remember about the hand is  that it is a little bit more sensitive  to the patient  and these veins because they are so  superficial  they do like to roll so it's very  important that you stabilize  the iv site before you stick it because  whenever i'm talking about rolling what  i'm meaning is that  whenever something goes to penetrate the  skin it can easily cause that vein just  to go to the side like this and just  watch my finger touch this thing see  how that goes to the side like that if  we put a needle  there with our iv we would hit it and  then the pain would just go to the side  and you would just be in the tissue so  it's very important you have the patient  just  you know tighten their hand a little bit  and then you go and stabilize it by  pulling the skin like that before you  stick  so when you have found a vein to stick  you want to make sure that you ask  yourself okay is this vein good enough  number one  is this vein going to hold this iv gauge  that i've selected like  if you're going to be putting in a large  gauge let's say an 18 gauge  you don't want to go in a really small  vein because  it's probably going to blow so you want  to make sure you pick a good enough size  vein  also look at how that vein is designed  is it  nice and straight try to find veins that  are straight because  if they curve around they may be hard to  thread that plastic cannula into  so try to select a nice straight one and  pick a site that is feasible to your  patient now i know  a lot of times whenever you're taking  care of patients in the hospital  you're just going to use whatever vein  you can get because that patient is so  sick and they don't have a lot to choose  from  but if you do have some things to choose  from try to choose something that's good  for your patient  like the ac if your patient's going to  have a lot of fluids running  you don't want to probably go here  because naturally your patient wants to  bend their arm they're going to be  eating they're going to be moving around  the bed  and if they have fluids running when you  bend the arm  your pump's going to be because it's  occluded you're going to have to go fix  it or it's going to drive your patient  crazy  so also that site can be very painful  because they're naturally bending their  arm as well so just keep those things in  mind so i know what vein i'm going to  use  on this patient to start an iv so i'm  going to go ahead and just remove this  tourniquet  temporarily and set everything up okay  so that wraps up this video and if you  would like to watch  more videos on nursing skills you can  access the link in the youtube  description below
 

